FIX DOCS — rationale for every task in fix.prompt
================================================

This file is a copy of fix.prompt in which every task additionally carries:
  WHY THIS TASK EXISTS          the problem, how it shows up, how it was found
  WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES the concrete outcome after the fix
followed by the task exactly as it appears in fix.prompt.

fix.prompt is authoritative for task status ([ ] / [x] / [-]) and for the
Decision:/Result: lines; the checkboxes below are a snapshot of the copy and
are not maintained.  If the two files ever disagree about what a task
should do, fix.prompt wins and this file should be regenerated.

Source: configuration review of 2026-10-24 (Emacs 31.1, branch astra_fix).
Companion file: fix.prompt — authoritative task list and status.


HOW TO USE THIS FILE (instructions for the agent)
-------------------------------------------------

1. Read AGENTS.md first and follow it: `dk/` prefix, lexical binding,
   non-package functions in src/dk-functions.el, `dk/*` variables in
   src/dk-vars.el, global keys in src/dk-keymap.el, package-specific keys in
   that package's `use-package` form, named hook helpers instead of lambdas.
   Match the surrounding comment density and style.

2. Pick the FIRST task whose heading starts with `## [ ]`:
       grep -n '^## \[ \]' fix.prompt | head -1
   Do only that one task in this session unless the user says "continue".

3. Tasks tagged [ASK] contain a decision that belongs to the user.  Before
   writing any code, ask the listed question (AskUserQuestion, recommended
   option first), then write the answer into that task's `Decision:` line.
   If the user picks "skip", mark the task `## [-]` with the reason in
   `Result:` and move on.

4. Line numbers in tasks are hints from the review snapshot.  Earlier tasks
   may have moved code: always locate the code by its content.

5. Implement the change.  Update comments that the change makes untrue.
   If a keybinding or user-visible behaviour changes, update cheatsheet.md.
   If an external tool requirement changes, update install.md.

6. Verify (both the generic checks below and the task's own `Verify:`).
   Run batch checks in a SANDBOX COPY of the config, never in
   ~/.config/emacs itself, so history/recentf/places are not rewritten.
   Re-create the sandbox after editing files (it is a copy).

   Sandbox (use the session scratchpad directory if one exists, otherwise
   `mktemp -d`; never `rm -rf` a path built from a variable):

       SB=<fresh empty directory>
       cd ~/.config/emacs
       cp -a early-init.el init.el src themes test package-quickstart.el \
             package-quickstart.elc elpa "$SB"/
       [ -f custom.el ]     && cp -a custom.el "$SB"/
       [ -d tree-sitter ]   && cp -a tree-sitter "$SB"/

   Smoke test (must print "startup OK", inspect every warning):

       cd "$SB" && emacs --batch --init-directory="$SB" \
         --eval '(setq native-comp-jit-compilation nil)' \
         --load early-init.el --eval '(package-activate-all)' \
         --load init.el --eval '(message "startup OK")'

   ERT suite (append any new test/*.el files):

       cd "$SB" && emacs --batch --init-directory="$SB" \
         --eval '(setq native-comp-jit-compilation nil)' \
         --load early-init.el --eval '(package-activate-all)' \
         --load init.el -l ert \
         -l test/dk-functions-test.el -l test/dk-integration-test.el \
         -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit

   Byte-compile warnings (should report nothing new):

       cd "$SB" && emacs --batch --init-directory="$SB" \
         --eval '(setq native-comp-jit-compilation nil)' \
         --load early-init.el --eval '(package-activate-all)' \
         --load init.el --eval '(setq byte-compile-warnings t)' \
         --eval '(dolist (f (directory-files "src" t "\\.el\\'"'"'")) (byte-compile-file f))'

   Delete the .elc files the last command leaves in "$SB/src" (they are in
   the sandbox only).  Interactive-only behaviour (GUI keys, window layout,
   popups) cannot be fully checked in batch: say so and list the manual
   steps for the user.

7. Mark the task done: change its heading `## [ ]` to `## [x]` and fill in
   `Result:` with one or two lines (files touched, what was verified,
   anything left for manual testing).  Do not edit other tasks' status.

8. Do not commit unless the user asks.  Each task suggests a commit subject
   in the repository's imperative style.  Never commit fix.prompt/fix.docs
   changes mixed into a config commit unless the user asks.

9. Stop and report: what changed, verification output summary, manual
   checks for the user, and the ID of the next open task.


STATUS LEGEND:  [ ] open   [x] done   [-] skipped by user decision


==========================================================================
P1 — BUGS AND HAZARDS
==========================================================================

## [ ] T01 · Fix the haskell-ts REPL command (and its false-passing test)

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
The REPL keys are the main bridge from a haskell-ts-mode source buffer to an
interactive GHCi. Both Haskell packages define a function with the same
name, `run-haskell`: haskell-mode's (through inf-haskell) starts a
cabal/stack session, haskell-ts-mode's starts a plain GHCi. Whichever file
loads last owns the name. The config therefore copies haskell-ts-mode's
implementation into a private name, `dk/haskell-ts-run-repl`, at the moment
haskell-ts-mode loads, so the ts-mode keys keep starting the ts-mode REPL
even after the classic package is loaded later. The copy is guarded with
`fboundp` so that re-loading dk-languages (after inf-haskell has replaced
`run-haskell`) does not overwrite a good copy with the classic one.

The guard is defeated by use-package itself. For a deferred package, `:bind`
turns every bound command into an autoload that points at the package file,
and does so before the package is loaded. An autoload object counts as
`fboundp`. When haskell-ts-mode finally loads and `:config` runs, the guard
believes the alias already exists and skips the `defalias`. The symbol stays
an autoload pointing at a file that is now loaded and never defines it.
Pressing C-c C-z makes Emacs load the file again looking for the definition,
which fails with "Autoloading file .../haskell-ts-mode.elc failed to define
function dk/haskell-ts-run-repl". The review confirmed this: the function
cell was `(autoload "haskell-ts-mode" nil t nil)` after loading, and calling
it raised exactly that error.

The existing integration test still passed. It only checks that the function
cell is the same object before and after a reload. An autoload object is
stable, so the comparison holds, and the test never checks that the command
can actually be called. A test that cannot fail for the bug it is named
after gives false confidence, which is why the task also strengthens it.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- C-c C-z and C-c C-r work in haskell-ts-mode again and start the tree-
  sitter mode's GHCi.
- The original reload safety is kept: once a real function has been
  captured, later reloads do not replace it.
- The test asserts what matters (the command is a real, callable function).
  The task requires confirming that it fails without the fix, so it actually
  guards against this regression.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-languages.el (~58-67), test/dk-integration-test.el (~4-14)
Problem: `:bind` creates an autoload for `dk/haskell-ts-run-repl` before
`:config` runs, so `(unless (fboundp 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl) (defalias ...))`
never fires.  C-c C-z / C-c C-r in haskell-ts-mode signal "Autoloading file
.../haskell-ts-mode.elc failed to define function dk/haskell-ts-run-repl".
Change:
  - Define the alias when the symbol is unbound OR still an autoload:
      (unless (and (fboundp 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl)
                   (not (autoloadp (symbol-function 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl))))
        (defalias ...))
    Keep the existing intent: once a real function is captured, a reload of
    dk-languages after inf-haskell is loaded must not replace it.
  - Update the comment to say why the autoload case must be handled.
  - Strengthen the integration test: after `(require 'haskell-ts-mode)` assert
    `(functionp (symbol-function 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl))` and
    `(not (autoloadp (symbol-function 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl)))`, both before
    and after `(require 'inf-haskell)` and the `(load "dk-languages" nil t)`.
Verify: ERT passes; in batch after `(require 'haskell-ts-mode)`,
`(autoloadp (symbol-function 'dk/haskell-ts-run-repl))` is nil.  Revert the
fix temporarily (mentally or in the sandbox) to confirm the new test fails
without it.
Commit: "Fix haskell-ts REPL alias shadowed by its autoload"
