## [ ] T02 · Stop splitting Cargo/cabal/Go workspaces into per-package projects

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
project.el finds the project for a file by walking up the directory tree and
stopping at the FIRST directory that holds either a VC marker (.git) or one
of `project-vc-extra-root-markers`. Because the closest match wins, listing
Cargo.toml, *.cabal and go.mod makes every package directory a project root,
even when all of them sit in one git repository. A Cargo workspace has a
Cargo.toml in every member crate. A multi-package cabal project has a .cabal
file in every package. Go monorepos often nest modules with their own
go.mod.

Eglot asks project.el for the project and starts one language server per
(project, language) pair. Opening files from three crates of one workspace
therefore starts three rust-analyzer processes. Each one discovers the real
workspace root through cargo metadata and indexes the WHOLE workspace
anyway, so you pay three times the memory, CPU and startup time for the same
information. HLS, which is very memory-hungry, behaves the same with cabal
packages. With `eglot-autoshutdown` and a long-lived systemd daemon, those
processes live as long as any of their buffers.

Project commands are narrowed too. C-x p f lists only one crate's files, C-x
p g (consult-ripgrep) searches only that crate, consult-project-buffer shows
only that crate's buffers, and `project-switch-project` remembers each crate
as a separate project. The current comment says this splitting is "what you
want in a cabal or cargo workspace", but that is the opposite of how
workspace-aware LSP servers expect to be rooted. The review reproduced it:
in a scratch workspace, crates/a and crates/b got two different roots.

The replacement markers (cabal.project, stack.yaml, Cargo.lock, go.work)
exist only at workspace level. They still give a directory outside git a
project root, which is the legitimate reason to have extra markers at all,
without splitting workspaces. compile_commands.json stays because it
normally lives at the top of a C/C++ tree, or is symlinked there. The trade-
offs are small and spelled out in the task.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- One language server per workspace instead of one per package: less memory
  and CPU, faster startup, and no duplicated indexing.
- Project-wide commands (find file, ripgrep, buffer list) cover the whole
  workspace.
- Eglot and the project commands agree on what "the project" is.
- Directories outside git still get a project root through workspace-level
  markers.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-navigation.el (~63-67)
Problem: `project-vc-extra-root-markers` contains "Cargo.toml", "*.cabal" and
"go.mod"; the nearest marker wins, so every workspace member becomes its own
project and Eglot starts one LSP server per member.
Change:
  - Replace per-package markers with workspace-level ones:
      '("cabal.project" "stack.yaml" "Cargo.lock" "go.work"
        "compile_commands.json")
  - Rewrite the comment above it: markers exist to give NON-git directories a
    root; inside git the repository root (or a workspace file) is the root,
    because Eglot uses the project root as the server root.  Mention the
    trade-off (a lone crate outside git becomes a project after its first
    build creates Cargo.lock; a lone cabal package outside git needs a
    cabal.project).
Verify: in a scratch git repo with Cargo.toml at the top and crates/a,
crates/b each containing Cargo.toml + src/lib.rs, `(project-root
(project-current nil "<repo>/crates/a/src/"))` returns the repo root (it
returned crates/a/ before the change).  Smoke test + ERT pass.
Commit: "Root projects at workspace level, not per package"
Result:


## [ ] T03 · Stop hiding git-tracked files with the ignored-directory list

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
The ignored-directory list was introduced as a safety net for projects that
do not gitignore their own build output. But all three mechanisms it feeds
apply unconditionally, including to files git tracks:
- `project-vc-ignores` becomes a git pathspec exclusion
  (`:(exclude,glob,top)**/build/`), which removes matching paths from the
  `git ls-files` output that C-x p f uses, tracked files included.
- `grep-find-ignored-directories` prunes every directory with that name, at
  any depth, in rgrep and `project-find-regexp`.
- ripgrep's `--glob=!build` excludes every path component named `build`,
  whether it is a file or a directory. Globs passed on the command line
  override ripgrep's gitignore logic.

Generic names like build, dist and vendor often hold real, tracked content:
build/ directories with scripts and Dockerfiles in Go and JS repos, a
deliberately checked-in Go vendor/ tree, dist/ folders in some packages.
When tracked files disappear from find-file and from project search, you get
silent misses. A search reports "no matches" for something that exists, a
wgrep refactor misses occurrences, and find-file cannot find a file you know
is there. Silent incompleteness in search is worse than extra noise, because
nothing tells you to look again. The review confirmed it: a tracked
build/tool.sh was missing from `project-files`.

Dropping the generic names keeps the net for directories that are
unambiguously generated (dist-newstyle, .stack-work, .hie, target, cmake-
build-*, .cache, node_modules, .direnv). Making the ripgrep globs directory-
only (trailing slash, gitignore semantics) stops rg from skipping ordinary
FILES that happen to share one of those names. A project that really needs
more exclusions can set `project-vc-ignores` in its own .dir-locals.el, so
project-specific knowledge stays with the project instead of being imposed
on every repository.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- File lists and project searches include every tracked file again, so
  search results can be trusted.
- The safety net still catches genuine build output in projects that do not
  ignore it.
- There is a documented, per-project way to add more exclusions.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-vars.el (~48-79), src/dk-navigation.el (~68-80, ~98-107),
       src/dk-completion.el (~147-159)
Problem: `dk/project-ignored-directories` excludes any directory named
build, dist, vendor (…) at any depth from C-x p f, rgrep and consult-ripgrep,
even when git tracks it (verified: tracked build/tool.sh vanished from
`project-files`).  ripgrep's `--glob=!NAME` also excludes plain FILES of
that name.
Change:
  - Remove the ambiguous generic names "build", "dist" and "vendor" from the
    list.  Keep names that are unambiguously generated: "dist-newstyle"
    ".stack-work" ".hie" "target" "cmake-build-*" ".cache" "node_modules"
    ".direnv".
  - Make the ripgrep globs directory-only: format them as
    `--glob=!%s/` (gitignore semantics: trailing slash = directories only).
  - Update the dk-vars.el docstring: what the list is for, why generic names
    are excluded, and that a project needing more can set
    `project-vc-ignores` in its own .dir-locals.el (the variable is
    `safe-local-variable` for lists).
Verify: in a scratch git repo with tracked build/tool.sh, `project-files`
includes it.  In a scratch dir containing target/x.txt (directory) and a
file literally named `target`, run rg with the resulting
`consult-ripgrep-args` globs: target/x.txt is skipped, the file `target` is
searched.  Smoke test + ERT pass.
Commit: "Narrow ignored build directories to generated ones"
