## [ ] T05 · Make helpful reuse its window and let q close it

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
helpful calls `helpful-switch-buffer-function` every time it shows
something, including when you follow a cross-reference link inside a helpful
buffer. The current function always splits the selected window. From inside
a helpful window it splits the helpful window again, so browsing
documentation produces a cascade of ever-smaller windows until the frame is
too small to split. The review confirmed 3 windows after two lookups.

Second problem: a window made with `split-window` and filled with `set-
window-buffer` does not get the `quit-restore` window parameter that
`display-buffer` records. `quit-window`, which `q` runs in helpful, uses
that parameter to decide whether to delete the window. Without it, `q` just
shows some other buffer in that window and the split stays. Every lookup
therefore leaves permanent layout debris behind, which is probably why ESC
deleting all windows (T04) felt useful.

`display-buffer` with an action list is Emacs' standard way to say where a
buffer should appear:
- `display-buffer-reuse-mode-window` reuses a helpful window that is already
  visible, so there is at most one documentation pane.
- `display-buffer-in-direction` keeps the existing preference: split right
  when the window spans the full frame width, otherwise below.
- `display-buffer-same-window` is the fallback for a frame too small to
  split, which matches today's fallback.
`display-buffer` does not select the new window, so point stays where you
were typing, as it does now. It records `quit-restore`, so `q` deletes the
pane and the layout returns to what it was. Following links inside a helpful
buffer replaces its content in place, like a browser.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- At most one helpful pane, reused for every lookup.
- `q` closes the pane and restores the previous layout.
- Following links inside helpful no longer creates windows.
- The existing preferences are kept (right/below split, point stays in the
  code window), and there is no longer any reason to rely on ESC to clean
  up.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-functions.el (~55-72), src/dk-ui.el (~111-121)
Problem: `dk/helpful-open` splits on every call, including following links
inside a helpful buffer (verified: 2 lookups -> 3 windows).  The window is
filled with `set-window-buffer`, so it has no quit-restore parameter and
`q` cannot delete it.
Change: rewrite `dk/helpful-open` around `display-buffer`:
  - In a helpful buffer: `(pop-to-buffer-same-window buf)` (link navigation
    replaces the content in place).
  - Elsewhere:
      (display-buffer
       buf `((display-buffer-reuse-mode-window
              display-buffer-in-direction
              display-buffer-same-window)
             (mode . helpful-mode)
             (direction . ,(if (window-full-width-p) 'right 'below))))
    i.e. reuse a visible helpful window, otherwise split right/below as
    before, otherwise use the current window.  Point stays in the original
    window when a split is made (display-buffer does not select).
  - Update the docstring and the dk-ui.el comment.
Verify (batch, count `window-list`): from a code buffer -> 2 windows; call
again from the helpful window -> still 2; call again from the code window
-> still 2 (reused); `quit-window` in the helpful window -> 1 window.
Manual: C-h f, follow a link, press q.
Commit: "Reuse the helpful window and let q close it"
