## [ ] T06 · Give embark its keys back in Org/Markdown (flyspell conflict)

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
Minor-mode keymaps take precedence over the global keymap. `flyspell-mode-
map` binds C-. to `flyspell-auto-correct-word` and C-; to `flyspell-auto-
correct-previous-word`. With `flyspell-use-meta-tab` at its default t, it
also takes C-M-i (M-TAB). Embark's two main entry points, `embark-act` and
`embark-dwim`, are bound to C-. and C-; globally. flyspell is turned on in
every Org and Markdown buffer whenever a spell checker is installed, which
is the case on this machine. The review confirmed all three bindings.

In prose buffers, then, C-. does not open embark's action menu. It silently
replaces the word at point with a spelling suggestion. The same key works
one way everywhere else and edits your text in prose, which is both
inconsistent and destructive. C-M-i is the standard completion-at-point key,
and in prose it also autocorrects instead of completing.

Removing only those bindings keeps every other flyspell feature:
highlighting, C-, to jump to the next error, C-c $ to correct the word
before point, and the mouse menu. `flyspell-use-meta-tab` has to be set
before flyspell builds its keymap at load time, and use-package's `:custom`
does exactly that.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Embark works on the same keys in every buffer.
- No more accidental autocorrections from a key you meant for something
  else.
- M-TAB completes in prose buffers.
- Spell checking itself is unchanged and still reachable.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-writing.el (~47-61)
Problem: `flyspell-mode-map` binds C-. and C-; (auto-correct), shadowing the
global `embark-act` / `embark-dwim` in every prose buffer; it also takes
C-M-i (completion-at-point) via `flyspell-use-meta-tab`.
Change in the flyspell `use-package` form:
  - `:custom (flyspell-use-meta-tab nil)` (must be set before flyspell loads;
    :custom does that).
  - `:config` with `(keymap-unset flyspell-mode-map "C-." t)` and
    `(keymap-unset flyspell-mode-map "C-;" t)`.
  - Comment: auto-correct stays reachable through `C-c $`
    (flyspell-correct-word-before-point) and M-x; C-, (next error) is kept.
Verify: after `(require 'flyspell)` in the loaded config,
`(keymap-lookup flyspell-mode-map "C-.")`, "C-;" and "C-M-i" are nil.  In a
temp org buffer with flyspell-mode on, `(key-binding (kbd "C-."))` is
`embark-act`.  (If no spell checker is installed the form is skipped
entirely; then check with the guard bypassed in the sandbox.)
Commit: "Stop flyspell from shadowing embark keys"
Result:


## [ ] T07 · Stop apheleia from reformatting Haskell and Elisp unasked  [ASK]

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
`apheleia-global-mode` formats every buffer on save whose major mode has an
entry in `apheleia-mode-alist`. The defaults, confirmed in the review,
include haskell-mode → fourmolu, haskell-ts-mode → fourmolu and emacs-lisp-
mode → lisp-indent. The config already recognized this danger for C/C++:
without project configuration, clang-format would rewrite every C file into
LLVM style, so it is gated on a .clang-format file being present.

Haskell has the same problem in a worse form. Haskell projects choose
between ormolu, fourmolu with its own configuration, stylish-haskell, or no
formatter, and fourmolu's defaults differ from ormolu's. Saving a file in a
project that does not use fourmolu rewrites the whole file. That produces
huge diffs in code review, merge conflicts with collaborators, and
formatting churn mixed in with your real change. If fourmolu is not
installed, every save produces an apheleia error instead.

For Emacs Lisp, lisp-indent re-indents the whole buffer on every save. That
includes third-party package sources you reach with M-. and files in other
people's repositories. Its result depends on the indentation rules known to
your session (for example each macro's `declare indent`), which may differ
from the author's environment, so it creates spurious changes.

`apheleia-inhibit-functions` is apheleia's official hook for this. Putting
all the rules in one inhibit function gives one policy, "a formatter runs
only when the project opts in", instead of special cases. fourmolu.yaml is
fourmolu's own project configuration file, so its presence is a clear opt-
in. `lisp-data-mode` is the parent of emacs-lisp-mode and lisp-interaction-
mode, so one check covers both. Go and Rust are untouched because gofmt and
rustfmt are the universal standard in their ecosystems. This is an [ASK]
task because formatting policy is personal: some people want their own
Haskell formatted always.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Saving a file never rewrites it in a style the project did not choose, so
  diffs contain only intended changes.
- One documented opt-in rule for formatters across all languages.
- No error noise when a formatter is not installed in projects that do not
  use it.

