## [ ] T10 · Fix file completion styles (TRAMP + orderless for recent files)

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
`completion-category-overrides` completely replaces `completion-styles` for
the given category. The `file` category covers find-file, dired prompts,
consult-recent-file (C-x C-r) and other file prompts. With only partial-
completion configured there, two things go wrong:
- TRAMP host-name completion (/ssh:ho TAB) depends on the `basic` style
  being tried first. Both the vertico and the orderless documentation say
  so. partial-completion alone can fail to complete methods and host names.
- orderless is switched off in all of those prompts. C-x C-r has hundreds of
  full paths, and orderless is what lets you type "emacs init" in any order.
  partial-completion needs the pieces in order and treats a space as a
  literal character, so "emacs init" matches nothing.

The global `completion-styles '(orderless basic)` shows the intent was
orderless everywhere, with file prompts as a special case for paths. Styles
are tried in order and the first one that produces matches wins:
- `basic` (exact prefix): what you get when typing a path normally, and what
  TRAMP needs.
- `partial-completion`: /u/s/l expansion, plus mid-component matching on
  Emacs 31 because the leading-wildcard option is already set.
- `orderless`: the fallback for free-form, multi-word queries.
Nothing that works today stops working; queries that fail today start to
succeed.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- TRAMP host and method completion is reliable.
- Recent-file search is as flexible as every other prompt.
- File prompts and other prompts behave consistently.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-completion.el (~86-93)
Problem: `completion-category-overrides '((file (styles partial-completion)))`
drops `basic` (needed first for TRAMP host completion) and disables
orderless for every file-category prompt, including C-x C-r.
Change: `'((file (styles basic partial-completion orderless)))`, and rewrite
the comment: basic first for TRAMP and literal prefixes, partial-completion
for /u/s/l paths, orderless last as fallback for space-separated words
(e.g. "emacs init" in consult-recent-file).
Verify: `completion-category-overrides` value after load, then in batch:
    (completion-all-completions
     "emacs init"
     (lambda (s p a)
       (if (eq a 'metadata) '(metadata (category . file))
         (complete-with-action a '("~/.config/emacs/init.el" "~/x/y.el") s p)))
     nil 10)
must return the init.el entry.  Manual: C-x C-r "emacs init"; C-x C-f
"/ssh:" offers hosts.
Commit: "Restore basic and orderless styles for file prompts"
Result:


## [ ] T11 · Stop org rendering snake_case as subscripts

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
`org-pretty-entities-include-sub-superscripts` makes org display `a_b` as
"a" with a subscript "b". `org-use-sub-superscripts` defaults to t
(confirmed), which means ANY underscore after a word character starts a
subscript. In technical notes that mangles snake_case identifiers, file
names like my_file.txt and environment variables like FOO_BAR: the
underscore disappears and the rest is lowered and shrunk. org-appear reveals
the raw text only while the cursor is inside it, so the document misreads
everywhere else. Export to HTML or LaTeX produces the same subscripts,
because `org-export-with-sub-superscripts` also defaults to t.

Setting both options to `'{}` means only explicit forms like a_{1} and x^{2}
are treated as sub/superscripts. That is org's documented idiom for
technical writing, and math notation stays fully available.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Identifiers, paths and variable names display and export literally.
- Intentional subscripts and superscripts still work, written with braces.
- The existing org-appear setting for sub/superscripts stays meaningful.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-writing.el (~75-77)
Problem: `org-pretty-entities-include-sub-superscripts t` with the default
`org-use-sub-superscripts t` turns `file_name` into file + subscript "name".
Change: add `(org-use-sub-superscripts '{})` and
`(org-export-with-sub-superscripts '{})` to the org `:custom` block with a
comment: only braced forms a_{1}, x^{2} are rendered/exported.
Verify: values after `(require 'org)`.  Manual: "file_name" shows literally,
"a_{1}" renders as subscript.
Commit: "Render org sub/superscripts only in braces"
