## [ ] T22 · Make missing tree-sitter grammars visible; fix the grammar test  [ASK]

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
dk-treesit.el makes tree-sitter optional. When a grammar is missing it falls
back to the classic mode, or to text-mode for file types that have no
classic mode installed. That resilience is good, but at review time NO
grammars were installed on this machine: there was no ~/.config/emacs/tree-
sitter directory and nothing on the system library path. The consequences:
- .go files open in text-mode, with no syntax highlighting, no gopls (the
  Eglot hook is on go-ts-mode) and no gofmt on save.
- go.mod, YAML, CMake and Dockerfile files also open in text-mode.
- C/C++ use the classic modes (still fine with Eglot), and rustic runs on
  classic rust-mode.

The only signal is one line in *Messages* at startup. Under the daemon,
startup messages end up in the journal or in a *Messages* buffer nobody
reads. `display-warning` sends the notice to *Warnings*, the standard
channel for "part of your setup is degraded", where it actually gets
noticed.

The integration test `dk/installed-grammars-select-working-modes` fails only
because grammars are missing, an environmental failure. A suite that is
always red trains you to ignore it and hides real failures. `skip-unless`
reports the test as skipped with the reason instead.

Installing the grammars uses the config's own `dk/treesit-install-all`. It
writes only to the git-ignored tree-sitter/ directory but needs network
access and a C compiler, hence the [ASK]. It runs in the real configuration
directory because that is where the grammars must end up: the single,
explicit exception to the sandbox rule.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- A degraded tree-sitter setup is visible instead of silent.
- The test suite is meaningful again: green, or explicitly skipped with a
  reason.
- Optionally, Go support and the tree-sitter modes start working on this
  machine.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-treesit.el (~99-103), test/dk-integration-test.el (~28-43)
Problem: no grammars are installed (~/.config/emacs/tree-sitter missing), so
.go/go.mod/YAML/CMake/Dockerfile open in text-mode (no gopls, no gofmt), C
and Rust use classic modes, and the only signal is a *Messages* line.  The
integration test then fails for environmental reasons.
Question: "Should I also install the pinned grammars now (network + C
compiler, writes ~/.config/emacs/tree-sitter, git-ignored)?"
  1. (Recommended) Yes, run dk/treesit-install-all in batch after the code change.
  2. No, only the code/test change.
Decision:
Change:
  - Replace the startup `message` with `(display-warning 'dk-treesit
    (format ...) :warning)` so it lands in *Warnings*.
  - In `dk/installed-grammars-select-working-modes`, `skip-unless` all
    grammars needed by the cases are available, so the test reports
    "skipped" instead of failing on machines without grammars.
  - If option 1: run in the REAL init dir (this is the one sanctioned
    exception, it only writes the git-ignored tree-sitter/ dir):
      emacs --batch --init-directory="$HOME/.config/emacs" \
        --load early-init.el --eval '(package-activate-all)' \
        --load init.el --eval '(dk/treesit-install-all)'
Verify: smoke test shows the warning when grammars are absent; ERT shows the
grammar test skipped (absent) or passed (installed).
Commit: "Warn about missing tree-sitter grammars"
