## [ ] T23 · Ask before recursive deletes on remote dired

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
`dired-recursive-deletes 'always` means deleting a directory never asks
whether to recurse. Locally, `delete-by-moving-to-trash t` makes that safe:
everything goes to the freedesktop trash and can be recovered. That safety
net does not exist over TRAMP. Emacs cannot move remote files into your
local trash, so deletion on a remote host is a permanent recursive delete.
On a server, D on a directory plus one y/n confirmation (which lists names
and is easy to confirm by reflex) permanently removes the whole tree,
possibly important data.

With `'top`, dired asks explicitly "Recursively delete X?" for each top-
level directory, with an option to answer for all of them. The setting is
made buffer-locally from `dired-mode-hook`, only in remote dired buffers.
dired reads the variable in the dired buffer during deletion, so the local
value is honoured and local behaviour is unchanged. The note about `--group-
directories-first` is documentation only: it is a GNU ls option, and dired
over TRAMP to BSD or macOS hosts would fail with it. That matters only if
you work on such hosts.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- The local workflow is unchanged, with the trash still the safety net.
- Remote recursive deletion needs an explicit confirmation, which lowers the
  risk of irreversible loss.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-functions.el, src/dk-navigation.el (~25-41)
Problem: `dired-recursive-deletes 'always` relies on the trash, but over
TRAMP there is no trash: D permanently deletes directory trees after one
prompt.
Change: add `dk/dired-remote-safety` (dk-functions.el): when
`(file-remote-p default-directory)`, `(setq-local dired-recursive-deletes
'top)`.  Hook it on `dired-mode-hook` from the dired `use-package`.
Comment: locally the trash makes 'always safe; remotely it is permanent.
(Optional, only if the user dired's into BSD/macOS hosts: note in the
comment that `--group-directories-first` needs GNU ls remotely.)
Verify: in a dired-mode temp buffer with a remote-looking
`default-directory` stubbed via cl-letf on `file-remote-p`, the hook sets
the local value to `top`; a local dired buffer keeps `always`.
Commit: "Confirm recursive deletes in remote dired"
Result:


## [ ] T24 · Sane kill-ring/search-ring persistence  [ASK]

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
savehist writes the kill ring (up to 200 entries as configured) in plain
text to ~/.config/emacs/history. The kill ring holds everything you kill or
copy, including clipboard content Emacs picks up on yank. That can include
passwords, API tokens and other secrets copied from a password manager or a
terminal. Persisted, they survive restarts and stay on disk indefinitely.
The file is mode 600, but it ends up in backups, dotfile syncs and disk
images.

Killed text also keeps its text properties (font-lock faces and the like).
savehist writes them out as `#("text" 0 5 (face ...))`, which bloats the
file and slows down reading it at startup. The history file was already 48
KB at review time.

The caps are misleading too. savehist trims each list to the given length,
but `kill-ring-max` (120) and `search-ring-max` (16) already trim the live
rings (confirmed), so the configured 200 never applies. Setting each ring
size once, as the single source of truth, makes savehist store exactly the
ring. Stripping properties at save time keeps the file small and readable.
The trade-off, that live entries lose properties such as yank handlers at
each save, is documented. This is an [ASK] task because persisting clipboard
history is a privacy decision.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Persisting the kill ring becomes a deliberate privacy choice.
- The size settings mean what they say.
- A smaller, cleaner history file.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-defaults.el (~46-56), src/dk-functions.el
Problem: the kill-ring is saved in plain text to `history` (anything
copied, passwords included) with font-lock text properties (file bloat),
and the savehist caps (200) exceed the ring sizes (kill-ring-max 120,
search-ring-max 16), so the numbers mean nothing.
Question: "Keep persisting the kill-ring across restarts?"
  1. (Recommended) Yes, but strip text properties and keep caps consistent.
  2. No, persist only search rings.
Decision:
Change:
  - Set the ring sizes explicitly once (e.g. kill-ring-max 200,
    search-ring-max 100, regexp-search-ring-max 100) and register the
    variables in `savehist-additional-variables` without separate caps.
  - Option 1: add `dk/savehist-strip-kill-ring` to `savehist-save-hook`:
    replace kill-ring with `substring-no-properties` copies of its string
    entries.  Docstring notes the trade-off (live entries lose properties
    such as yank handlers at each save).
  - Option 2: drop kill-ring from the list.
Verify: variable values; run `dk/savehist-strip-kill-ring` on a kill-ring
containing a propertized string -> entries have no properties.
Commit: "Keep savehist ring limits consistent"
Result:


## [ ] T25 · Move lock files out of project trees; fix .gitignore gaps

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
Lock files: when you modify a buffer, Emacs creates a symlink named
.#filename next to the file, to warn other Emacs sessions that edit the same
file. These links appear in project directories:
- `git status` shows them as untracked. That is why this repository's
  .gitignore needs `.#*`, and every other repository needs it too.
- File watchers (cargo watch, vite or webpack, ghcid or HLS, test runners,
  Syncthing or Dropbox) see a new file and rebuild, or trip over the
  dangling symlink. Some tools crash on broken symlinks.
Emacs 28 added `lock-file-name-transforms` to relocate them, the same
mechanism this config already uses for auto-save files. Locking keeps
working between local Emacs sessions, because every session applies the same
transform.

.gitignore gaps:
- autosave/, the directory behind `dk/auto-save-directory`, is not ignored.
  `git status` was clean at review time only because the directory was
  empty. As soon as an auto-save exists (crash-recovery copies of unsaved
  edits), it shows up as untracked and could be committed by accident.
- *.elc files written by `dk/recompile-config` would also show up as
  untracked. AGENTS.md says build output stays out of source control.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- No lock-file noise in projects, git status or file watchers.
- The repository cannot accidentally commit auto-saves, lock files or
  bytecode.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-vars.el, src/dk-defaults.el (~13), .gitignore
Problem: `create-lockfiles t` writes .#file symlinks next to edited files
(git status noise, file-watcher triggers).  Also `autosave/` (used by
`dk/auto-save-directory`) and `*.elc` (made by `dk/recompile-config`) are
not git-ignored.
Change:
  - `dk/lock-directory` in dk-vars.el ("locks/" under user-emacs-directory,
    trailing slash, docstring like the auto-save one).
  - In dk-defaults.el: `make-directory` it and set
    `lock-file-name-transforms` to `((".*" ,dk/lock-directory t))`.
    Comment: locking still protects against other local Emacs sessions; for
    TRAMP files cross-machine lock detection is lost (acceptable).
  - .gitignore: add `autosave/`, `locks/`, `*.elc`.
Verify: in the sandbox, visit a temp file, modify it, check that the lock
appears under $SB/locks/ and not beside the file.
Commit: "Keep lock files out of project directories"
Result:


## [ ] T26 · Back up version-controlled files too  [ASK]

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
The config sets up numbered backups: three versions per file, collected in
one directory. But `vc-make-backup-files` is nil (the default, confirmed),
so files under version control never get a backup; Emacs assumes the VCS is
your backup. Nearly everything you edit lives in git, so the backup
machinery covers only a few files outside any repository.

Git protects only committed states. Uncommitted work is not recoverable from
git: the hours between commits, a bad wgrep replacement across many files,
an unwanted reformat on save, an accidental kill followed by a save. A
backup is made at the first save of each session, which captures the file as
it was before the session, exactly the version you want after a mistake. The
cost is one small file per edited file per session, pruned automatically to
three. If you decide git is enough, the task still adds a comment saying so,
so that the config does not appear to protect more than it does.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Backups cover what the configuration appears to promise.
- Uncommitted work has a safety net, or the decision not to have one is
  documented.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-defaults.el (~15-24)
Problem: `vc-make-backup-files` is nil, so the numbered-backup setup never
covers git-tracked files — most of what is edited.
Question: "Also keep numbered backups of git-tracked files (first save per
session, local files only)?"
  1. (Recommended) Yes.
  2. No — git is the backup (skip, but add a comment saying so).
Decision:
Change: `(setq vc-make-backup-files t)` next to the backup settings with a
comment; or only the comment for option 2.
Verify: value after load.
Commit: "Back up version-controlled files"
Result:


## [ ] T27 · Clean recentf and save it periodically under the daemon

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
recentf records every visited file, including package sources opened with
M-. into elpa/, magit's COMMIT_EDITMSG, temporary files, and files inside
the backup and auto-save directories. They crowd C-x C-r and the recent-file
part of consult-buffer, and push real files out of the 300-entry list.
`recentf-exclude` is the built-in filter for exactly this.

Under the systemd daemon there is a second problem. recentf writes its list
only from `kill-emacs-hook`, and the daemon runs for days or weeks. If it
crashes, is killed by the OOM killer, or the machine loses power, every file
visited in that session is gone from recentf. savehist has its own auto-save
timer; recentf does not. An idle timer that saves every 300 seconds closes
that gap. `inhibit-message` keeps "Wrote .../recentf.eld" out of the echo
area. Keeping the timer in a dk/ variable and cancelling it before creating
a new one means reloading the module does not stack duplicate timers, which
follows the reload-safety principle in AGENTS.md.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- Recent-file lists contain files you actually work on.
- The list survives daemon crashes.
- The module can be reloaded safely.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: src/dk-vars.el, src/dk-navigation.el (~13-21)
Problem: no `recentf-exclude`, so elpa sources, COMMIT_EDITMSG, /tmp files
and backup/autosave files fill C-x C-r; under the systemd daemon recentf is
only written on a clean exit, so a crash loses the session's list.
Change:
  - `recentf-exclude` in `:custom`: regexps for the elpa directory
    (`package-user-dir`), `dk/backup-directory`, `dk/auto-save-directory`,
    `temporary-file-directory`, and "/COMMIT_EDITMSG\\'"
    (use `regexp-quote` on directory names).
  - A periodic save: `dk/recentf-save-timer` (defvar in dk-vars.el) holding
    `(run-with-idle-timer 300 t #'dk/recentf-save-quietly)`; cancel an
    existing timer first so reloading the module does not stack timers.
    `dk/recentf-save-quietly` (dk-functions.el) calls `recentf-save-list`
    with `inhibit-message` bound to t.
Verify: `recentf-exclude` value; `(recentf-include-p
"<elpa>/foo/foo.el")` nil, ordinary file t; reloading dk-navigation twice
leaves exactly one timer (`timer-idle-list`).
Commit: "Exclude noise from recentf and save it periodically"
Result:


## [ ] T28 · Document Customize precedence

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
--------------------
init.el loads custom.el before the modules so that Customize values for the
`dk/*` variables take effect: `defvar` does not override a value that
already exists. But the modules then set many options themselves with `setq`
and `:custom`. Any such option changed through M-x customize is overwritten
again at the next startup. The Customize UI shows it as saved, you believe
it applies, and after a restart it is back. Changing the load order would
break the `dk/*` mechanism. The current init.el comment does mention the
precedence, but in a way that is easy to miss. Documentation is the fix with
no behaviour trade-off: it tells you, and future you, where each kind of
setting has to be changed.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING IT ACHIEVES
-----------------------------
- No more confusion about why a customization does not stick.
- A clear, discoverable rule for where to change settings.

TASK (as in fix.prompt)
-----------------------

Files: init.el (~27-36 comment), cheatsheet.md (or install.md)
Problem: custom.el loads before the modules, so a value set with M-x
customize for a variable a module also sets is silently overwritten at the
next startup.
Change: documentation only — make the init.el comment state the rule
plainly ("Customize works only for options no module sets; to change those,
edit the module or a dk/* variable") and add the same one-paragraph note to
cheatsheet.md.
Verify: smoke test.
Commit: "Document Customize precedence"
